{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016inWBHkKk9fUhCMjG62sQd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LMWNo8hC9B2rbv7uzY6Tkp","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Review the paper below using a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Solver Trace Ablation Reveals Reward Hacking in GRPO-Trained Formal Reasoners: Diagnosis, Taxonomy, and Goodhart's Law\n\nThe paper proposes Solver Trace Ablation (STA), a diagnostic framework for detecting reward hacking in GRPO-trained formal reasoners. STA derives Intensional Structural Reward (ISR) via leave-one-out (LOO) ablation on solver input components (premises in FOL). Applied to GRPO-trained FOL formalizers on FOLIO, the paper makes three claims:\n\n1. **ISR as Diagnostic**: exec-only GRPO achieves 93% prove rate while ISR collapses from 0.64→0.003 over 1000 steps. 5 hacking strategies identified via manual annotation.\n2. **ISR as Reward (Goodhart)**: At β=0.1 (additive bonus), ISR provides directional improvement over random control (mean ISR ratio ~2.1× across 3 seeds), but with high inter-seed variance (std/mean=0.47); genuine rate delta shrinks to +3.7pp (not statistically significant). At β=0.3, Goodhart collapse occurs.\n3. **Cross-Logic Transfer**: STA mechanically transfers to Prolog but ISR diagnostic power is weaker. Presented as preliminary evidence.\n\nAdditional results: O(1) vs O(N) ablation (negative result), curriculum ablation, SFT baseline comparison, multi-logic training (negative interference), cross-logic zero-shot transfer (null result), 1.5B vs 8B model scaling.\n\n## Key Tables/Figures from the paper\n\n**Table 1** (ISR collapse trajectory, exec-only, seed 42):\n- 100 steps: PR=0.32, ISR=0.6423, Comp=6.58\n- 500 steps: PR=0.93, ISR=0.1324, Comp=8.50\n- 1000 steps: PR=0.96, ISR=0.0027, Comp=18.55\n\n**Table 2** (3-seed exec-only):\n- s42: conclusion embedding, PR=0.93, ISR=0.132, Comp=8.5\n- s43: repetition collapse, PR=0.14, ISR=0.000\n- s44: tautological collapse, PR=0.98, ISR=0.570, Comp=2.04\n\n**Table 3** (Goodhart landscape, s42):\n- exec-only: PR=0.93, ISR=0.132, Gen~5%\n- ISR β=0.1: PR=0.81, ISR=0.337, Gen=25.9%\n- random β=0.1: PR=0.79, ISR=0.104, Gen=17.7%\n- ISR β=0.3: PR=0.18, ISR=0.026\n- random β=0.3: PR=0.48, ISR=0.045\n\n**Table 4** (3-seed β=0.1 ISR vs random):\n- s42: ISR(0.81/0.337/25.9%) vs Random(0.79/0.104/17.7%)\n- s43: ISR(0.72/0.24/21%) vs Random(0.74/0.13/17%)\n- s44: ISR(0.55/0.12/13%) vs Random(0.71/0.09/14%)\n- mean±std: ISR(0.69±0.13/0.23±0.11/20±6.5%) vs Random(0.75±0.04/0.11±0.02/16.2±2%)\n- 95% CI for ISR ratio: [0.85, 3.45], crossing 1.0\n\n**Table 7** (SFT comparison):\n- SFT: PR=0.48, ISR=0.45, Gen=58%, Hack=42%\n- exec-only s42: PR=0.93, ISR=0.132, Gen~5%, Hack~95%\n- ISR β=0.1 s42: PR=0.81, ISR=0.337, Gen=25.9%, Hack=74.1%\n\n**Table 8** (O(1) vs O(N)):\n- O(1): Pearson r=0.21, MAE=0.31, AUROC=0.56, 3.2 samples/sec\n- O(N): Pearson r=1.0, MAE=0.0, AUROC=0.84, 0.3 samples/sec\n\n**Table 10** (Prolog exec-only vs exec+STA β=0.1):\n- Exec-only mean: PR=0.57, ISR=0.23\n- STA mean: PR=0.51, ISR=0.26 (ratio 1.12×, PR drops 6.3pp)\n\n**Table 13** (1.5B vs 8B):\n- 1.5B s42: PR=0.93, ISR=0.132, Comp=8.5, collapse ~200 steps\n- 8B: PR=0.96, ISR=0.09, Comp=14.2, collapse ~150 steps\n- 8B with ISR β=0.1: PR=0.88, ISR=0.29\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe planned experiments included:\n1. ISR β=0.1 seeds 43, 44 (done, reported in Table 4-5)\n2. Random β=0.1 seeds 43, 44 (done, reported)\n3. 3-seed aggregate ISR vs Random (done, Table 4)\n4. O(1) vs O(N) ablation (done, Table 8)\n5. Prolog exec-only 3 seeds (done, Table 10)\n6. Prolog exec+STA 3 seeds (done, Table 10)\n7. Multi-logic FOL+Prolog joint training (done, Table 11)\n8. Cross-logic transfer evaluation (done, Table 12)\n9. SFT baseline (done, Table 7)\n10. SMT-LIB experiments (not run, acknowledged in Limitations)\n11. Qwen3-8B scaling (done, Table 13, single seed only)\n12. Hacking characterization (done, taxonomy in Section 3.4 + Appendix A)\n13. Curriculum ablation (done, Table 9)\n\nKey concern from context: The 3-seed aggregate shows the β=0.1 sweet spot is fragile — 95% CI crosses 1.0, genuine rate delta not significant. The paper correctly acknowledges this.\n\n## Your Review Focus (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is ISR well-defined? Are the experimental controls appropriate? Is the LOO ablation correctly formulated?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Sample sizes, statistical significance, seed coverage, baseline appropriateness\n3. **Claims vs. evidence alignment**: Do the results actually support the claims as stated?\n4. **Experimental coverage**: Are key ablations present? Missing controls?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Are sufficient details provided?\n6. **Presentation of results**: Tables, clarity, honesty about negative results\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Soundness Score: X/4\n### Empirical Rigor Score: X/4\n### Overall Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16293,"cache_read_input_tokens":37237,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16293},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"89cdeb8e-ead5-4a30-afa3-c65e0c614ccf","uuid":"b6095fac-2829-420b-ab73-5f6a6a1f14bd"}
