{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AsSXaPjEDcMHsB8zBubh3X","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012a5UjScXWpBt5tXfVxKpGz","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__read_project_file","input":{"project":"solver-trace-ablation","file":"literature_map.yaml","max_chars":0},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6556,"cache_read_input_tokens":39908,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6556,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":63,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LMWNo8hC9B2rbv7uzY6Tkp","session_id":"89cdeb8e-ead5-4a30-afa3-c65e0c614ccf","uuid":"c90dead2-3640-4e0d-826d-70c9762d5566"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012a5UjScXWpBt5tXfVxKpGz","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_3/projects/solver-trace-ablation/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Literature Map for Solver Trace Ablation (STA)\\\\n# Categories:\\\\n#   1. neurosymbolic-rl: Neurosymbolic reasoning + RL/GRPO\\\\n#   2. reward-shaping: Reward shaping for code/formal verification\\\\n#   3. mutation-testing: Mutation testing in software engineering\\\\n#   4. intensional-analysis: Intensional program analysis\\\\n#   5. reward-hacking: Reward hacking / overoptimization\\\\n#   6. fol-formalization: FOL/Prolog formalization with LLMs\\\\n#   7. goodhart-overoptimization: Goodhart's Law theory and dose-response dynamics\\\\n#   8. verification-reward: Solver/prover as RL reward signal\\\\n\\\\npapers:\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # Category 1: Neurosymbolic reasoning + RL/GRPO\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n  - id: deepseek-r1\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"DeepSeek-AI\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2501.12948 / Nature 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: neurosymbolic-rl\\\\n    role: method-component\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Demonstrates that pure RL (GRPO) can incentivize reasoning in LLMs without SFT.\\\\n      STA builds on the same GRPO framework but replaces binary outcome reward with\\\\n      intensional structural reward for neurosymbolic formalization.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      DeepSeek-R1-Zero trained via GRPO alone achieves 71% on AIME 2024,\\\\n      showing emergent chain-of-thought reasoning from RL alone.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: deepseekmath\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"DeepSeekMath: Pushing the Limits of Mathematical Reasoning in Open Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Zhihong Shao, Peiyi Wang, Qihao Zhu, Runxin Xu, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2402.03300\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: neurosymbolic-rl\\\\n    role: method-component\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Introduces GRPO (Group Relative Policy Optimization), the RL algorithm\\\\n      used in STA training. GRPO eliminates the critic model by estimating\\\\n      baselines from group scores.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      GRPO matches PPO performance while significantly reducing training\\\\n      resources; improves GSM8K 82.9->88.2% and MATH 46.8->51.7%.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: logic-rl\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Logic-RL: Unleashing LLM Reasoning with Rule-Based Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Xian et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2502.14768\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: neurosymbolic-rl\\\\n    role: direct-competitor\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Uses rule-based RL (REINFORCE++) for LLM logical reasoning on synthetic\\\\n      logic puzzles. Closest competitor to STA's approach of RL for logical\\\\n      formalization, though Logic-RL uses extensional (answer-correctness)\\\\n      rewards rather than STA's intensional structural rewards.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      REINFORCE++ outperforms GRPO on logic puzzles with superior stability;\\\\n      rule-based reward design is critical for RL-based logical reasoning.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: mellgren-prolog-grpo\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Training Language Models to Use Prolog as a Tool\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Niklas Mellgren\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2512.07407\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: neurosymbolic-rl\\\\n    role: direct-competitor\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Uses GRPO to train a 3B LLM to generate Prolog code for reasoning,\\\\n      directly comparable to STA's approach of GRPO-training a formalizer.\\\\n      Key difference: uses pass/fail reward vs STA's structural ISR.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      GRPO is effective for teaching small LLMs to use Prolog as a tool\\\\n      for logical and mathematical reasoning.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: satlm\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"SatLM: Satisfiability-Aided Language Models Using Declarative Prompting\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Xi Ye et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2023\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: neurosymbolic-rl\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Neurosymbolic approach where LLM generates declarative specifications\\\\n      verified by SAT solver. Similar pipeline to STA (LLM->formal spec->solver)\\\\n      but uses prompting, not RL training.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Declarative formulation outperforms imperative program-aided LMs by 23%\\\\n      on GSM; achieves SoTA on LSAT and BoardgameQA.\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # Category 2: Reward shaping for code/formal verification\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n  - id: lightman-verify-step\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Let's Verify Step by Step\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Hunter Lightman, Vineet Kosaraju, Yura Burda, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2023\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2305.20050 / ICLR 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-shaping\\\\n    role: method-component\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Establishes that process supervision (step-level reward) outperforms\\\\n      outcome supervision (final-answer reward) for reasoning. STA's ISR\\\\n      is analogous: it provides structural process-level signal about which\\\\n      components of a formalization matter, rather than just pass/fail.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Process-supervised reward model achieves 78% on MATH, significantly\\\\n      outperforming outcome-supervised models. Released PRM800K dataset.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: reward-shaping-rlhf\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Reward Shaping to Mitigate Reward Hacking in RLHF\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Jiayi Fu, Xuandong Zhao, Chengyuan Yao, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2502.18770\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-shaping\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Studies reward shaping design principles for RLHF: bounded rewards and\\\\n      rapid-then-convergent growth. STA's ISR follows these principles\\\\n      naturally since structural necessity scores are bounded [0,1].\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Two key reward shaping principles: (1) bounded RL reward, (2) rapid\\\\n      initial growth with gradual convergence. PAR method achieves 5%+\\\\n      win rate improvement on AlpacaEval 2.0.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: rlvr-reasoning\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards Implicitly Incentivizes Correct Reasoning in Base LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2506.14245\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-shaping\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Shows that binary verifiable rewards (pass/fail) can implicitly incentivize\\\\n      reasoning. STA argues that for neurosymbolic formalization, binary reward\\\\n      is insufficient and structural reward is needed to avoid reward hacking.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      RLVR with binary verification implicitly teaches correct reasoning\\\\n      strategies, not just answer matching.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: rubric-grpo\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Rubric-Grounded Reinforcement Learning: Structured Judge Rewards for Generalizable Reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2605.08061\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-shaping\\\\n    role: direct-competitor\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Uses structured rubric-based rewards with GRPO, similar in spirit to\\\\n      STA's structured ISR. Key difference: rubric rewards come from LLM\\\\n      judges, while ISR comes from solver-based mutation testing.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Structured multi-dimensional reward signals improve GRPO training\\\\n      generalization compared to scalar rewards.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: gao-effective-rl-reward\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"On Designing Effective RL Reward at Training Time for LLM Reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Jiaxuan Gao, Shusheng Xu, Wenjie Ye, Weilin Liu, Chuyi He, Wei Fu, Zhiyu Mei, Guangju Wang, Yi Wu\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2410.15115\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-shaping\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Demonstrates that learned process/outcome reward models can hurt RL\\\\n      training, producing worse performance than simple success reward only.\\\\n      Directly explains plan005 NEGATIVE: STA's ISR as auxiliary process\\\\n      reward degrades prove_rate compared to exec-only baseline.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      PRM/ORM rewards may NOT help or even hurt RL training vs success-only\\\\n      reward. Solution: Clipping and Delta techniques to bound accumulated\\\\n      reward magnitude prevent reward hacking.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: prof-process-outcome\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Beyond Correctness: Harmonizing Process and Outcome Rewards through RL Training\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Chenlu Ye, Zhou Yu, Ziji Zhang, Hao Chen, Narayanan Sadagopan, Jing Huang, Tong Zhang, Anurag Beniwal\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2509.03403\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-shaping\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Shows that naively combining PRM+ORM signals leads to performance\\\\n      degradation. PROF consistency filter retains only PRM-ORM consistent\\\\n      samples, achieving 4%+ improvement over blending. Suggests STA should\\\\n      only apply ISR when consistent with execution outcome.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Process rewards frequently suffer from inaccuracies and reward hacking\\\\n      susceptibility. PROF filters training data by PRM-ORM consistency,\\\\n      outperforming naive reward blending by 4%+ accuracy.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: moskovitz-constrained-rlhf\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Confronting Reward Model Overoptimization with Constrained RLHF\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Ted Moskovitz, Aaditya K. Singh, DJ Strouse, Tuomas Sandholm, Ruslan Salakhutdinov, Anca D. Dragan, Stephen McAleer\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2023\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2310.04373 / ICLR 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-shaping\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      First study on overoptimization in composite reward models. Shows that\\\\n      fixed-weight linear scalarization of component rewards leads to\\\\n      degradation past a threshold. Proposes constrained RL with dynamic\\\\n      Lagrange multiplier weighting as alternative to fixed ISR weight.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Correlation between component RMs significantly affects overoptimization\\\\n      thresholds. Constrained RL with Lagrange multipliers outperforms\\\\n      fixed-weight reward composition.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: skalse-reward-hacking\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Defining and Characterizing Reward Hacking\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Joar Skalse, Nikolaus H. R. Howe, Dmitrii Krasheninnikov, David Krueger\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2022\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2209.13085 / NeurIPS 2022\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-shaping\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Formal proof that for stochastic policies, two reward functions are\\\\n      unhackable only if one is constant. Theoretically explains why any\\\\n      non-zero ISR weight can decrease prove_rate -- this is a fundamental\\\\n      property of reward composition, not STA-specific.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Any non-constant proxy reward can decrease true reward when optimized.\\\\n      Unhackability requires the proxy to be constant, making all non-trivial\\\\n      auxiliary rewards theoretically vulnerable to degrading primary performance.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: progrs-outcome-conditioned\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LLM Reasoning with Process Rewards for Outcome-Guided Steps\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Mohammad Rezaei, Jens Lehmann, Sahar Vahdati\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2604.02341\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-shaping\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      PRM scores optimized as absolute rewards amplify fluent failure modes.\\\\n      Outcome-conditioned centering shifts incorrect trajectories' PRM scores\\\\n      to zero mean, preserving ranking info without corrupting gradients.\\\\n      Suggests STA should condition ISR on execution outcome.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Naive process rewards induce reward hacking by rewarding locally fluent\\\\n      but globally incorrect reasoning. Outcome-conditioned centering resolves\\\\n      the conflict with fewer rollouts than outcome-only baselines.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: qwen-prm-lessons\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"The Lessons of Developing Process Reward Models in Mathematical Reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Zhenru Zhang et al. (Qwen Team, Alibaba)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ACL 2025 Findings (arXiv 2501.07301)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-shaping\\\\n    role: supporting-evidence\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      MC estimation-synthesized PRM data leads to quality degradation; policy\\\\n      model generates \\\\\\\"answer-correct but reasoning-flawed\\\\\\\" responses, causing\\\\n      process reward / outcome misalignment. Directly parallels ISR problem:\\\\n      structurally correct but semantically wrong proof traces get high ISR\\\\n      but low execution reward.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      PRM performance depends critically on data quality; MC estimation\\\\n      introduces systematic bias. Answer-correct reasoning-flawed samples\\\\n      corrupt process supervision signal.\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # Category 3: Mutation testing in software engineering\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n  - id: demillo1978\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Hints on Test Data Selection: Help for the Practicing Programmer\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Richard A. DeMillo, Richard J. Lipton, Frederick G. Sayward\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 1978\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Computer 11(4):34-43\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: mutation-testing\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Foundational paper introducing mutation testing. STA's component\\\\n      necessity testing directly extends this idea: instead of mutating\\\\n      source code to test test suites, STA mutates logical formulas\\\\n      to test component necessity for solver success.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Introduced mutation testing and the coupling effect hypothesis:\\\\n      tests that detect simple faults also detect complex faults.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: offutt-coupling\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Investigations of the Software Testing Coupling Effect\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"A. Jefferson Offutt\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 1992\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ACM TOSEM 1(1):5-20\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: mutation-testing\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Empirically validates the coupling effect hypothesis that underpins\\\\n      mutation testing. STA's single-component ablation is analogous to\\\\n      first-order mutation; the coupling effect suggests this suffices.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Test data killing all first-order mutants killed >99% of second-\\\\n      and third-order mutants, validating the coupling effect.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: jia-harman-survey\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"An Analysis and Survey of the Development of Mutation Testing\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Yue Jia, Mark Harman\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2011\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"IEEE TSE 37(5):649-678\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: mutation-testing\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Comprehensive survey of mutation testing covering three decades of\\\\n      development. Provides theoretical and practical foundation for STA's\\\\n      adaptation of mutation testing to logical formulas.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Systematic classification of mutation operators, cost reduction\\\\n      techniques, and equivalence problem -- all relevant to STA's design\\\\n      of clause-level ablation operators.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: just-mutants-real-faults\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Are Mutants a Valid Substitute for Real Faults in Software Testing?\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Rene Just, Darioush Jalali, Laura Inozemtseva, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2014\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"FSE 2014\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: mutation-testing\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Validates that mutation testing (structural/intensional analysis of\\\\n      program adequacy) correlates with real fault detection. Supports STA's\\\\n      premise that structural analysis of formalization components provides\\\\n      meaningful signal about formalization quality.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Mutant detection is statistically correlated with real fault detection,\\\\n      and mutation score is a better predictor of test effectiveness than\\\\n      code coverage.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: llmorpheus\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LLMorpheus: Mutation Testing Using Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Frank Tip, Jon Bell, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"IEEE TSE 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: mutation-testing\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Uses LLMs to generate realistic code mutants beyond traditional\\\\n      mutation operators. Shows mutation testing evolving toward\\\\n      ML-integrated approaches, validating STA's cross-pollination\\\\n      of mutation testing ideas into ML training.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      LLM-generated mutants simulate real bugs that rule-based operators\\\\n      cannot produce, improving mutation testing realism.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: mutation-guided-meta\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Mutation-Guided LLM-based Test Generation at Meta\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Christopher Foster et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"FSE 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: mutation-testing\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Industrial application of mutation-guided LLM test generation at\\\\n      Meta. Demonstrates that mutation analysis can guide LLM behavior\\\\n      in production -- analogous to how STA uses mutation (ablation) to\\\\n      guide GRPO reward signal.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Mutation-guided approach generates tests that kill significantly\\\\n      more mutants than unguided LLM test generation at Meta scale.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: mutation-consistency-llm\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Mutation-based Consistency Testing for Evaluating the Code Understanding Capability of LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2401.05940\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: mutation-testing\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Uses code mutations to evaluate LLM code understanding consistency.\\\\n      Conceptually related to STA: both use systematic code/formula\\\\n      mutations to assess model capabilities.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      LLMs show significant inconsistency when evaluated on mutated code\\\\n      variants, suggesting superficial pattern matching.\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # Category 4: Intensional program analysis\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n  - id: deepmutation\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"A Probabilistic Framework for Mutation Testing in Deep Neural Networks\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2023\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Information and Software Technology 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: intensional-analysis\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Extends mutation testing to DNN testing with probabilistic mutation\\\\n      operators. Parallel to STA's extension of mutation testing from\\\\n      traditional programs to logical formulas.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Probabilistic mutation framework enables meaningful test adequacy\\\\n      assessment for neural networks through structural perturbation.\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # Category 5: Reward hacking / overoptimization\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n  - id: inform-reward-hacking\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"InfoRM: Mitigating Reward Hacking in RLHF via Information-Theoretic Reward Modeling\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-hacking\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Proposes information-bottleneck approach to detect and mitigate reward\\\\n      hacking. STA's ISR addresses the same problem differently: by grounding\\\\n      reward in verifiable structural properties (component necessity), it\\\\n      reduces the attack surface for reward hacking.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Variational information bottleneck filters irrelevant features from\\\\n      reward model; Cluster Separation Index detects reward overoptimization.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: mo-grpo\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"MO-GRPO: Mitigating Reward Hacking of Group Relative Policy Optimization on Multi-Objective Problems\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"CyberAgent AI Lab\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2509.22047\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-hacking\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Directly addresses GRPO reward hacking in multi-objective settings.\\\\n      STA's ISR can be viewed as adding a structural objective to the\\\\n      correctness objective, making MO-GRPO's insights directly applicable.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Standard GRPO normalizes after summing rewards, allowing high-variance\\\\n      objectives to dominate. MO-GRPO equalizes scales before aggregation.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: reward-hacking-survey\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Reward Hacking in the Era of Large Models: Mechanisms, Emergent Misalignment, Challenges\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2604.13602\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-hacking\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Comprehensive survey of reward hacking mechanisms in the LLM era.\\\\n      Provides theoretical framework for understanding why STA's structural\\\\n      reward is more robust than pure outcome-based reward.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Reward hacking manifests through specification gaming, reward model\\\\n      exploitation, and emergent misalignment; structural verification\\\\n      rewards are more robust than learned reward models.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: energy-loss-reward-hacking\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"The Energy Loss Phenomenon in RLHF: A New Perspective on Mitigating Reward Hacking\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-hacking\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Identifies energy loss in LLM final layer as an indicator of reward\\\\n      hacking during RL. Provides complementary diagnostic to STA's\\\\n      structural reward approach.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Energy loss in the final layer increases during RL and excessive\\\\n      increase characterizes reward hacking; EPPO algorithm penalizes\\\\n      this to mitigate hacking.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: spurious-rewards\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Spurious Rewards: Rethinking Training Signals in RLVR\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Ruixin Yang et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2506.10947 / NeurIPS 2025 submission\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-hacking\\\\n    role: direct-evidence\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Most directly relevant to STA findings. GRPO+random reward on\\\\n      Qwen2.5-Math improves 21.4%, nearly matching ground-truth reward's\\\\n      29.1%. Matches our plan017 finding: additive random (PR=48%) >\\\\n      additive ISR (PR=18%). Structured metrics can be worse than random\\\\n      noise due to systematic bias vs pure variance.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Random reward nearly matches ground-truth reward in GRPO training,\\\\n      suggesting GRPO's group normalization extracts signal from noise.\\\\n      Effect is model-dependent (works on Qwen2.5, not Llama3).\\\\n\\\\n  - id: gao-reward-overoptimization\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Scaling Laws for Reward Model Overoptimization\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Leo Gao, John Schulman, Jacob Hilton\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2023\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2023 (arXiv 2210.10760)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-hacking\\\\n    role: theoretical-foundation\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Foundational work quantifying Goodhart's effect: proxy reward rises\\\\n      but gold reward first rises then falls. ISR is a proxy reward that\\\\n      exhibits exactly this pattern — ISR can increase while prove_rate\\\\n      decreases, making it suitable for post-hoc diagnosis but harmful\\\\n      as training signal.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Gold/proxy reward divergence follows predictable scaling laws.\\\\n      Proxy reward optimization past critical point degrades gold reward,\\\\n      with the divergence point scaling with model and dataset size.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: karwowski-goodhart-rl\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Goodhart's Law in Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Jacek Karwowski, Oliver Hayman, Xingjian Bai, Klaus Kiendlhofer, Charlie Griffin, Joar Skalse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2024 (arXiv 2310.09144)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-hacking\\\\n    role: theoretical-foundation\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Geometric interpretation of Goodhart's Law in MDPs: optimizing\\\\n      imperfect proxy past critical point decreases true objective. ISR as\\\\n      proxy has partial correlation with execution correctness — exceeding\\\\n      critical optimization point pushes policy toward high-ISR but\\\\n      low-correctness regions. Explains plan002/plan005 negative results.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Formalizes Goodhart's Law for RL: any non-trivially correlated proxy\\\\n      reward creates regions where proxy and true reward diverge, and\\\\n      policy optimization inevitably enters these regions.\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # Category 6: FOL/Prolog formalization with LLMs\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n  - id: folio\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"FOLIO: Natural Language Reasoning with First-Order Logic\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Simeng Han et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2022\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2209.00840 / EMNLP 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: fol-formalization\\\\n    role: evaluation\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Primary FOL reasoning benchmark. STA uses FOLIO-style NL->FOL\\\\n      formalization as the target task for GRPO training. FOLIO's\\\\n      expert-annotated FOL annotations serve as reference formalizations.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      1,430 NL-FOL paired examples with reasoning depths up to 5+;\\\\n      GPT-4 still struggles on the hardest examples.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: proofwriter\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"ProofWriter: Generating Implications, Proofs, and Abductive Statements over Natural Language\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Oyvind Tafjord, Bhavana Dalvi, Peter Clark\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2021\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Findings of ACL-IJCNLP 2021\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: fol-formalization\\\\n    role: evaluation\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Source dataset for STA's training data. ProofWriter provides\\\\n      rule-based logical reasoning problems in natural language that\\\\n      STA uses for training formalizer models.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Generative model can reliably produce implications and proofs;\\\\n      accuracy exceeds prior methods by 9% and generalizes to unseen\\\\n      proof depths.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: logic-lm\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Logic-LM: Empowering Large Language Models with Symbolic Solvers for Faithful Logical Reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Liangming Pan, Alon Albalak, Xinyi Wang, William Wang\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2023\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Findings of EMNLP 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: fol-formalization\\\\n    role: direct-competitor\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      LLM->symbolic solver pipeline for logical reasoning, directly\\\\n      comparable to STA's formalizer->Prolog solver pipeline. Key\\\\n      difference: Logic-LM uses prompting, STA uses GRPO training.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      LLM+solver achieves 39.2% improvement over LLM-alone on logical\\\\n      reasoning benchmarks including ProofWriter and FOLIO.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: linc\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LINC: A Neurosymbolic Approach for Logical Reasoning by Combining Language Models with First-Order Logic Provers\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Theo X. Olausson, Alex Gu, Ben Lipkin, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2023\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"EMNLP 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: fol-formalization\\\\n    role: direct-competitor\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Neurosymbolic approach where LLM translates NL to FOL for theorem\\\\n      prover. Directly comparable to STA's NL->Prolog translation.\\\\n      LINC uses prompting-based approach; STA uses RL training.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      StarCoder+ (15.5B) with LINC outperforms GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 with\\\\n      CoT by 38% and 10% respectively on ProofWriter.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: fover-pei2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"FoVer: First-Order Logic Verification for Natural Language Reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Yu Pei, Yongping Du, Xingnan Jin\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"TACL 13:1340-1359\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: fol-formalization\\\\n    role: direct-competitor\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Uses LLM->FOL->Z3 pipeline for verifying NL reasoning correctness.\\\\n      Similar to STA's verification pipeline but focuses on post-hoc\\\\n      verification rather than training reward signal.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      FOL verification via Z3 significantly outperforms existing methods\\\\n      on ProofWriter and FOLIO benchmarks.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: lorp\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LoRP: LLM-based Logical Reasoning via Prolog\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Knowledge-Based Systems 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: fol-formalization\\\\n    role: direct-competitor\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      LLM->Prolog pipeline with FOL-to-Prolog translation mechanism.\\\\n      Closest to STA's formalization target (Prolog), though LoRP\\\\n      uses prompting while STA uses GRPO training with structural reward.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Systematic FOL-to-Prolog translation extends Prolog expressiveness;\\\\n      achieves SoTA accuracy on logical reasoning with three-vote majority.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: nl2fol\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"NL2FOL: Translating Natural Language to First-Order Logic for Logical Fallacy Detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Lalwani, Kim, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2405.02318\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: fol-formalization\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Step-by-step NL->FOL translation using LLMs with SMT solver\\\\n      verification. Demonstrates the NL->formal logic pipeline that\\\\n      STA aims to improve via RL training.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Neurosymbolic NL2FOL achieves 78% F1 on LOGIC dataset and\\\\n      generalizes to LogicClimate (80% F1), outperforming supervised\\\\n      models by 21%.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: logica2\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LogiQA 2.0: An Improved Dataset for Logical Reasoning in Natural Language Understanding\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Liu et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2023\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"IEEE/ACM TASLP 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: fol-formalization\\\\n    role: evaluation\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Logical reasoning evaluation benchmark derived from Chinese civil\\\\n      service exams. Provides supplementary evaluation for STA's\\\\n      formalization capabilities.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      8,678 multiple-choice questions covering categorical, disjunctive,\\\\n      and conditional reasoning; widely used LLM benchmark.\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # Category 7: Goodhart's Law theory and dose-response dynamics\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n  - id: manheim-goodhart-taxonomy\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Categorizing Variants of Goodhart's Law\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"David Manheim, Scott Garrabrant\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2019\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 1803.04585\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: goodhart-overoptimization\\\\n    role: theoretical-foundation\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Four-part Goodhart taxonomy: regressional, extremal, causal, adversarial.\\\\n      STA's high-β ISR collapse is best classified as extremal Goodhart\\\\n      (policy moves to OOD region where ISR↑ but prove_rate↓). Provides\\\\n      vocabulary for discussion section.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      First systematic categorization of Goodhart failure modes. Extremal\\\\n      variant occurs when proxy-goal relationship breaks in out-of-distribution\\\\n      regions reached by strong optimization.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: pan-reward-misspecification\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"The Effects of Reward Misspecification: Mapping and Mitigating Misaligned Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Alexander Pan, Kush Bhatia, Jacob Steinhardt\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2022\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2022\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: goodhart-overoptimization\\\\n    role: theoretical-foundation\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Across 9 environments, more capable agents exploit misspecifications more.\\\\n      Observes dose-dependent degradation — capability thresholds where true\\\\n      reward sharply drops. Matches STA's β=0.1→0.3 qualitative shift.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Reward misspecification causes dose-dependent degradation where true reward\\\\n      sharply drops past capability thresholds. More capable optimization\\\\n      produces worse outcomes under misspecification.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: kwa-catastrophic-goodhart\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Catastrophic Goodhart: Regularizing RLHF with KL Divergence Does Not Mitigate Heavy-Tailed Reward Misspecification\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Tat-Thang Kwa, Rachel Thomas, Adrià Garriga-Alonso\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: goodhart-overoptimization\\\\n    role: theoretical-foundation\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      If reward model error is heavy-tailed, policies achieve arbitrarily\\\\n      high proxy reward with no true utility gain. KL regularization provably\\\\n      fails. Explains why KL penalty in GRPO cannot prevent ISR collapse\\\\n      at high β if ISR error distribution is heavy-tailed.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      KL-regularized RL provably fails to prevent overoptimization under\\\\n      heavy-tailed reward misspecification. Policies can achieve arbitrarily\\\\n      high proxy reward with zero true utility.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: rafailov-daa-scaling\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Scaling Laws for Reward Model Overoptimization in Direct Alignment Algorithms\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Rafael Rafailov, Yaswanth Chittepu, Ryan Park, Harshit Sikchi, Joey Hejna, W. Bradley Knox, Chelsea Finn, Scott Niekum\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: goodhart-overoptimization\\\\n    role: supporting-evidence\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Extends Gao et al. to DPO/IPO/SLiC. Tests seven β values across\\\\n      1B-6.9B models. DAAs still overoptimize despite no explicit proxy RM.\\\\n      Validates β as a \\\\\\\"dose\\\\\\\" parameter — directly analogous to STA's\\\\n      ISR weight sweep.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Direct alignment algorithms exhibit overoptimization following\\\\n      predictable scaling laws. Smaller models overoptimize sooner.\\\\n      β-controlled dose-response is a general phenomenon across RL paradigms.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: huang-chi-squared\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Correcting the Mythos of KL-Regularization: Direct and Contrastive Distribution Correction\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Afra Feyza Akyürek, Banghua Zhu, Sham Kakade, Jiantao Jiao\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2025 Poster\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: goodhart-overoptimization\\\\n    role: supporting-evidence\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      KL-regularization is theoretically inadequate for preventing\\\\n      overoptimization. Chi-squared divergence provably alleviates it.\\\\n      Suggests that even with KL penalty in GRPO, STA's high-β collapse\\\\n      is theoretically expected.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Chi-squared preference optimization provably mitigates overoptimization\\\\n      where KL-regularization fails. Alternative divergence measures offer\\\\n      stronger guarantees.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: coste-rm-ensembles\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Reward Model Ensembles Help Mitigate Overoptimization\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Thomas Coste, Usman Anwar, Robert Kirk, David Krueger\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2023\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2023 Workshop on Alignment\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: goodhart-overoptimization\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Worst-case optimization across RM ensembles practically eliminates\\\\n      overoptimization for BoN and reduces it for PPO. Suggests ensemble\\\\n      of multiple solver metrics (instead of single ISR) as mitigation.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      RM ensembles with worst-case aggregation mitigate overoptimization.\\\\n      Ensemble size and diversity directly affect robustness.\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # Category 8: Solver/prover as RL reward signal\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n  - id: rlsf\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Reinforcement Learning via Symbolic Feedback\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Abhinav Jha, Shikhar Murty, Zico Kolter, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2405.16661\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: verification-reward\\\\n    role: direct-competitor\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Most directly relevant: uses solver/prover certificates as fine-grained\\\\n      token-level reward signal. Outperforms traditional approaches on program\\\\n      synthesis (+31.4%). STA extends this paradigm and shows when it breaks\\\\n      (Goodhart effects at high auxiliary reward weight).\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Symbolic reasoning tools provide poly-sized certificates as reward.\\\\n      Smaller RLSF models outperform GPT-4 on structured generation tasks.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: fover-kamoi2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"FoVer: Generalizable Process Reward Models via Formally Verified Training Data\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Ryo Kamoi, Yusen Zhang, Nan Zhang, Jiawei Han, Rui Zhang\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Findings of ACL 2026 (arXiv 2502.01345)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: verification-reward\\\\n    role: supporting-evidence\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Uses Z3 and Isabelle to synthesize PRM training data by annotating\\\\n      step-level error labels without human annotation. Same solver family\\\\n      as STA. Shows verification-based rewards work; STA extends by showing\\\\n      dose-response dynamics.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Formally verified training data enables generalizable process reward\\\\n      models competitive on ProcessBench and 12 reasoning benchmarks.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: deepseek-prover-v15\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"DeepSeek-Prover-V1.5: Harnessing Proof Assistant Feedback for Reinforcement Learning and Monte-Carlo Tree Search\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"DeepSeek AI\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2025 (arXiv 2408.08152)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: verification-reward\\\\n    role: method-component\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Uses GRPO with Lean 4 verification as binary reward. Same algorithm\\\\n      (GRPO) + formal verification, but binary (pass/fail). Binary reward\\\\n      avoids the graded-metric Goodhart risk that STA identifies with ISR.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      GRPO + binary Lean verification achieves 63.5% on miniF2F. Binary\\\\n      correctness guarantee via formal verification sidesteps proxy reward\\\\n      overoptimization.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: alphaproof\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"AlphaProof\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Google DeepMind\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Nature 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: verification-reward\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      AlphaZero-inspired RL with Lean proof verification at scale.\\\\n      Silver medal at IMO 2024. Binary verification reward avoids the\\\\n      Goodhart problem STA identifies with graded verification metrics.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Binary formal verification as RL reward scales to IMO-level\\\\n      mathematical reasoning. Demonstrates verification-reward RL works\\\\n      at the frontier of mathematical difficulty.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: math-shepherd\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Math-Shepherd: Verify and Reinforce LLMs Step-by-step without Human Annotations\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Peiyi Wang, Lei Li, Zhihong Shao, R.X. Xu, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ACL 2024 (arXiv 2312.08935)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: verification-reward\\\\n    role: supporting-evidence\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Automatically constructs process supervision data and uses it as\\\\n      step-by-step PPO reward. Automated verification reward without human\\\\n      labels, analogous to STA's automated ISR computation via solver.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Automated process supervision improves Mistral-7B on GSM8K\\\\n      (77.9→84.1%) and MATH (28.6→33.0%) without human annotation.\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # Additional reward-shaping papers (Goodhart survey additions)\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n  - id: bipars\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Learning to Utilize Shaping Rewards: A New Approach of Reward Shaping\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Yujing Hu, Weixun Wang, Hangtian Jia, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2020\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2020\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-shaping\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Bi-level optimization learns parameterized shaping reward weights.\\\\n      Can transform harmful shaping rewards into beneficial ones by learning\\\\n      negative weights. Directly addresses STA's problem: when ISR weight\\\\n      is wrong, automated weight learning could find optimal β.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Bi-level optimization automatically learns auxiliary reward weights,\\\\n      outperforming fixed weights and recovering from harmful shaping rewards.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: dapo\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"DAPO: An Open-Source LLM Reinforcement Learning System at Scale\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Qiying Yu et al. (ByteDance)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2503.14476\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: reward-shaping\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance: >\\\\n      Four techniques for stable multi-component reward in GRPO: Clip-Higher,\\\\n      Dynamic Sampling, token-level policy gradient, Overlong Reward Shaping.\\\\n      Addresses stability issues relevant to STA's multi-reward setup.\\\\n    key_finding: >\\\\n      Clip-Higher and Dynamic Sampling prevent entropy collapse in GRPO\\\\n      with multi-component rewards. Soft punishment for overlong outputs\\\\n      avoids reward hacking through length exploitation.\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 38485, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LMWNo8hC9B2rbv7uzY6Tkp","session_id":"89cdeb8e-ead5-4a30-afa3-c65e0c614ccf","uuid":"26df20b0-0d95-4648-a4c1-749396bc24ad","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:17:26.347Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AsSXaPjEDcMHsB8zBubh3X","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019yMXDcQd5MMYT2XrKok6f9","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__read_project_file","input":{"project":"solver-trace-ablation","file":"memory.md","max_chars":0},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6556,"cache_read_input_tokens":39908,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6556,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":63,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LMWNo8hC9B2rbv7uzY6Tkp","session_id":"89cdeb8e-ead5-4a30-afa3-c65e0c614ccf","uuid":"cfd938df-d2ee-451d-96ae-cf421dfb69c8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019yMXDcQd5MMYT2XrKok6f9","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_3/projects/solver-trace-ablation/memory.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# solver-trace-ablation — Memory\\\\n\\\\n## 环境信息 (2026-05-13)\\\\n\\\\n### westd-39279 服务器\\\\n- **GPU**: 2× RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell, 94GB VRAM 每张\\\\n- **CUDA**: 12.8, PyTorch 2.10.0+cu128\\\\n- **镜像**: aml-train（conda 环境，`source /root/miniconda3/etc/profile.d/conda.sh && conda activate base`）\\\\n- **ML 栈**: transformers/trl/vllm/accelerate 全部预装\\\\n- **Solvers**: Prover9 (LADR 2009-11A 源码编译), SWI-Prolog 8.4.2 (`/usr/bin/swipl`), Z3 4.16.0.0\\\\n- **模型**: Qwen3-4B 已下载（7.6GB，通过 ModelScope）\\\\n- **代码路径**: `/root/solver-trace-ablation/`\\\\n- **磁盘**: 系统盘 30GB (22G free)，数据盘 `/root/autodl-tmp/` 250GB (142G free)\\\\n\\\\n### 决策记录\\\\n- Qwen3-4B 用 ModelScope 下载（HuggingFace 直连可能慢）\\\\n- Prover9 从 LADR 源码编译（apt 无包）\\\\n- 94GB VRAM 单卡跑 Qwen3-4B 完全无压力\\\\n\\\\n## 数据 (2026-05-13)\\\\n\\\\n### 数据源变更：ProofWriter → FOLIO\\\\n- ProofWriter 原始数据无 FOL 列，改用 `tasksource/folio`（1204 条人工标注 FOL）\\\\n- Phase 0 子集 100 条，训练子集 500 条\\\\n- 34% 数据含 XOR (⊕) 符号，已在 `_normalize_fol()` 中修复展开\\\\n\\\\n### 数据约束 (2026-05-14, D009)\\\\n- **C001**: 所有 plan001/plan013 实验统一使用 `train_500_seed42.jsonl`，不再用 `proofwriter_fol_500.jsonl`（消除数据差异 confound）\\\\n\\\\n## Phase 0 关键发现 (2026-05-13)\\\\n\\\\n### Base Model ISR Baseline (sta-phase0-baseline)\\\\n- **Prove rate**: 12% overall, 18.2% excluding XOR-affected samples\\\\n- **Mean ISR**: 0.064 (very low — base model formalizations mostly unprovable)\\\\n- **ISR 区分度**: proved 样本 ISR 0.125–0.889\\\\n\\\\n### Ground-Truth ISR Baseline (sta-gt-isr-baseline)\\\\n- **GT prove rate**: 18% (limited by Prover9 only proving entailment; true=50%, false=4%, unknown=0%)\\\\n- **GT mean ISR**: 0.5588 (median 0.6125, range 0.14–1.00)\\\\n- **Model vs GT gap**: ISR 0.064 vs 0.559 = 8.7x 差距，STA 有强区分力\\\\n\\\\n### Prover9 局限性\\\\n- Prover9 只能证明 entailment（conclusion 从 premises 推出）\\\\n- `false` 和 `unknown` 类问题几乎无法证明（false 4%, unknown 0%）\\\\n\\\\n## Phase 1 关键经验 (2026-05-13)\\\\n\\\\n### fast_isr_fol 失败教训 (r4)\\\\n- `fast_isr_fol()` 是二值 tautology gate (0/1)，Qwen3-4B 不产生 tautology → ISR ≡ prove_rate → 两条件零差异\\\\n- **教训：binary ISR 无法提供有意义的 reward shaping 信号，必须用连续 ISR**\\\\n\\\\n### D010 Reward 公式转向 (2026-05-14)\\\\n- **旧公式（废弃）**: r = α·r_exec + β·r_exec·ISR, α+β=1 → ISR<1 时永远是 penalty\\\\n- **新公式**: r = r_exec · (1 + β·ISR), β>0 → ISR 只能 bonus，不能 penalty\\\\n\\\\n## Held-out eval_sta 全量结果 (2026-05-14) — 关键\\\\n\\\\n### 九组 eval 对比（含 plan016 1000步 + plan018 random β=0.1）\\\\n\\\\n| 实验 | Reward | Steps | eval PR | eval ISR | proved_only_ISR | n_components | 退化模式 |\\\\n|------|--------|-------|---------|----------|-----------------|--------------|----------|\\\\n| plan001-s42 | exec-only | 500 | **93%** | 0.123 | 0.132 | 6.58 | ✅ Genuine proofs |\\\\n| plan001-s43 | exec-only | 500 | **14%** | 0.000 | 0.000 | N/A | ❌ 长输出退化 |\\\\n| plan001-s44 | exec-only | 500 | **98%** | 0.570 | 0.582 | 2.04 | ❌ Tautological collapse |\\\\n| **plan016** | **exec-only** | **1000** | **96%** | **0.003** | **0.003** | **18.55** | **❌ 矛盾爆炸崩塌** |\\\\n| **plan002-b01** | **additive ISR β=0.1** | 500 | **81%** | **0.337** | **0.415** | **5.58** | **✅ Genuine + improved** |\\\\n| **plan018** | **additive random β=0.1** | 500 | **79%** | **0.104** | **0.131** | **8.46** | **⚠️ 冗余但无坍缩** |\\\\n| plan002-b03 | additive ISR β=0.3 | 500 | 18% | 0.026 | 0.143 | 10.67 | ❌ 过拟合+低泛化 |\\\\n| plan017 | additive random β=0.3 | 500 | 48% | 0.045 | 0.094 | 9.6 | ❌ 复杂但无用 |\\\\n| plan013 | convex random | 500 | 100% | 0.535 | 0.535 | 2.04 | ❌ Tautological hacking |\\\\n\\\\n### β=0.1 ISR vs Random 同剂量对照 (plan002-b01 vs plan018, D031 定论) — 重大发现\\\\n\\\\n**ISR β=0.1 提供 genuine structural guidance，非 variance regularization。**\\\\n\\\\n- PR 几乎相同 (0.81 vs 0.79) → β=0.1 噪声不影响证明能力\\\\n- ISR 差 3.2x (0.337 vs 0.104) → ISR 信号引导结构改善\\\\n- Components 5.58 vs 8.46 → ISR 组更精简\\\\n- random 组 36.7% proved 样本 ISR=0（无结构证明）\\\\n\\\\n### ISR-as-Reward β 剂量响应完整图谱\\\\n\\\\n| β | ISR reward PR | Random reward PR | ISR reward ISR | Random reward ISR | 结论 |\\\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\\\n| 0.1 | 0.81 | 0.79 | **0.337** | 0.104 | ISR >> random (3.2x) |\\\\n| 0.3 | 0.18 | 0.48 | 0.026 | 0.045 | ISR << random (Goodhart) |\\\\n\\\\n**剂量依赖**：低 β ISR 有结构价值；高 β ISR 触发 Goodhart 效应反而比 random 更差。\\\\n\\\\n### plan016 延长训练发现 (2026-05-14)\\\\n\\\\n**ISR 崩塌随训练持续恶化，无回升信号。**\\\\n- 500步 ISR=0.123 → 1000步 ISR=0.003 (-97.8%)\\\\n- Windowed ISR 单调递减：step 200 peak 0.1115 → step 1000 低至 0.0144\\\\n- 退化模式：非 2-component 同义反复，而是矛盾爆炸（大量无关前提嵌入矛盾）\\\\n- Components 6.58→18.55 (+182%)\\\\n- 论文意义：exec-only 延长训练是 reward hacking 恶化的干净消融证据\\\\n\\\\n### 跨 seed 退化分类 (2026-05-14)\\\\n\\\\n| Seed | 退化模式 | 特征 | 诊断信号 |\\\\n|------|----------|------|----------|\\\\n| s42 | ✅ Genuine | PR=93%, components=6.58 | 唯一健康 seed |\\\\n| s43 | ❌ Length explosion | PR=14%, 87% hit max_new_tokens | D029: 不重训 |\\\\n| s44 | ❌ Tautological collapse | PR=98%, components=2.04 | ISR=0.5 artifact |\\\\n\\\\n### 关键教训\\\\n1. **跨 seed 极不稳定**: exec-only reward landscape 方差极大\\\\n2. **ISR + component count 联合诊断**能区分 genuine proof vs tautological hack\\\\n3. **ISR-as-reward 有 sweet spot**: β=0.1 有效，β≥0.3 有害（Goodhart's Law）\\\\n4. **训练 PR vs eval PR 巨大鸿沟**: 训练 PR 不反映 held-out 泛化能力\\\\n5. **ISR β=0.1 vs random β=0.1**: ISR 3.2x 差异证明 ISR 信号有结构价值（非 variance regularization）\\\\n\\\\n## Downstream NLI 验证失败 (2026-05-14, plan014)\\\\n- Spearman ρ(ISR, correct) = -0.049 (p=0.187, 不显著)\\\\n- 根因：模型严重 true-bias (false accuracy=0%), NLI accuracy 天花板 34.1%\\\\n- D022/D029: 降级为 Limitation/Future Work\\\\n\\\\n## Goodhart 文献定位 (2026-05-14)\\\\n\\\\n### 核心 novelty（Goodhart 文献调研确认）\\\\n1. **首个在 neurosymbolic verification reward 上刻画 Goodhart 效应**：已有工作研究 RLHF preference RM (Gao et al. 2023)、game 环境 (Pan et al. 2022)、code execution (CodeRL) 的 reward hacking，无人研究 logical solver output 作为 RL reward 时的 Goodhart\\\\n2. **Verification-based auxiliary reward 的剂量响应曲线**：首个 β sweep 展示 solver-based metric (ISR) 的倒 U 型行为。已有 dose-response 研究在 RLHF preference (Rafailov et al. 2024) 或 robotics (Wang et al. 2025)，不在 formal verification\\\\n3. **Dose-dependent qualitative shift**：β=0.1→0.3 从 PR 81%→18% 的质变（非 smooth degradation），连接 Pan et al. 的 dose-dependent dynamics 但在 neurosymbolic 上下文。注意：仅 2 个 β 值，不足以称 \\\\\\\"phase transition\\\\\\\"，需更细粒度 β sweep\\\\n4. ~~Graded vs binary verification reward 的 Goodhart 易感性差异~~：**已降级为 discussion/future work**——无 binary ISR ablation 实验支撑，不能作为 novelty claim\\\\n\\\\n### Goodhart 分类\\\\n- 我们的 high-β ISR collapse = **extremal Goodhart** (Manheim & Garrabrant 2019)：policy 移到 ISR↑ 但 PR↓ 的 OOD 区域\\\\n- 可能有 **causal Goodhart** 成分：ISR 和 quality 的相关性在优化压力下断裂\\\\n\\\\n### 预期 reviewer 问题\\\\n1. \\\\\\\"Why not binary ISR?\\\\\\\" → binary 避免 Goodhart 但丢失更丰富训练信号，trade-off 引用 DeepSeek-Prover vs Lightman/Math-Shepherd\\\\n2. \\\\\\\"How relates to Gao et al. scaling laws?\\\\\\\" → β sweep 类似 KL sweep，可在其框架下画图\\\\n3. \\\\\\\"Why not adaptive β?\\\\\\\" → BiPaRS/ASR/ARS 已有 adaptive weighting，acknowledge 为 future work\\\\n4. \\\\\\\"Goodhart mechanism?\\\\\\\" → extremal + possible causal (Manheim taxonomy)\\\\n\\\\n### 文献资产\\\\n- `artifacts/literature_goodhart.md`: 30+ 篇论文，3 类别（Reward Overoptimization / Verification as Reward / Dose-Response）\\\\n- literature_map.yaml: 33 篇论文，6 类别\\\\n\\\\n## 文献空白与 Novelty (2026-05-14)\\\\n\\\\n### 核心 novelty（β=0.1 同剂量对照后更新）\\\\n- ISR 作为 neurosymbolic 结构质量的 **剂量敏感指标**——低剂量 bonus 提供 genuine structural guidance（3.2x vs random），高剂量触发 Goodhart\\\\n- ISR + component count 联合诊断区分 genuine vs degenerate proofs\\\\n- 文献中无人在 GRPO formal verification 场景做过此粒度的 reward shaping 消融\\\\n- **新增**：同剂量 ISR vs random 对照是干净的因果证据（β=0.1 唯一变量是 ISR vs random）\\\\n\\\\n### 关键文献\\\\n- Spurious Rewards (arXiv:2506.10947): random > structured reward，与我们 β=0.3 结果一致\\\\n- Gao et al. (ICML 2023): Goodhart effect scaling laws，解释 β 剂量依赖\\\\n- Karwowski et al. (ICLR 2024): Goodhart in RL 几何解释\\\\n\\\\n## Hacking 特征分析 (2026-05-14, plan012)\\\\n\\\\n### 操作性分类（6 种模式）\\\\n1. **Conclusion embedding** — 将 conclusion 嵌入 premises 中\\\\n2. **Repetition collapse** — 重复生成相同 premise\\\\n3. **Tautological collapse** — 退化为 2-component (premise=conclusion) 结构\\\\n4. **Complexity-without-utility** — 生成复杂但无用的 premises\\\\n5. **Premise padding** — 添加无关 premises 填充\\\\n6. **Conclusion Duplication with ISR Inflation** — 单 premise = conclusion，同时 game exec (P→P 永真) 和 ISR (唯一 premise necessary → ISR≈1.0)\\\\n\\\\n### Mode 6 详情: Conclusion Duplication with ISR Inflation (2026-05-14)\\\\n- **机制**: 模型发现 \\\\\\\"P→P provable + single necessary premise = ISR=1.0\\\\\\\" 捷径，同时 game exec 和 ISR 两个 reward\\\\n- **比 Mode 3 更隐蔽**: component_count 4-5（conclusion 有多 sub-components），不触发 2-component 检测\\\\n- **ISR β=0.1 特异性诱导**: s43 97% duplication, s44 65%+, s42 仅 16%（seed 依赖）\\\\n- **Random β=0.1 对照**: 0/79 samples duplication → ISR reward 结构性信号特异诱导此模式\\\\n- **与 Mode 3 定量区别**: Mode 3 comp=2 ISR≈0.5 | Mode 6 comp=4-5 ISR=0.8-1.0\\\\n- **检测信号**: single_premise + premise≈conclusion（非 comp_2_ratio）\\\\n\\\\n### 各条件 hacking 分布\\\\n- Exec-only s42: 87% conclusion-in-premises hack, 48% ISR=0\\\\n- Random reward: 100% tautology, 96% 2-component\\\\n- Additive ISR β=0.3: format collapse (79/82 无 Conclusion)\\\\n- Additive random β=0.3: 复杂但无用 (ISR≈0, n_necessary=0)\\\\n- **Additive ISR β=0.1: 健康分布, mean components=5.58, ISR=0.337**\\\\n- **Additive random β=0.1: 冗余但无坍缩, components=8.46, ISR=0.104, 36.7% ISR=0**\\\\n\\\\n## GPU 隔离教训\\\\n\\\\n### C002: 所有命令必须 `export CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=N`\\\\n- 必须在 `bash -c` 内部 export\\\\n- submit_training_job 命令格式: `bash -c \\\\\\\"export CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=N && ...\\\\\\\"`\\\\n\\\\n### bash -c 包装\\\\n- tmux 在 sh 下执行，source 是 bash built-in，必须用 `bash -c \\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"` 包装\\\\n\\\\n### Checkpoint 路径\\\\n- save_total_limit=1 时，模型直接保存在 output_dir 根目录\\\\n\\\\n### 磁盘\\\\n- **C003 约束**: `log_completions=False` + `save_total_limit=1`，output_dir 放 `/root/autodl-tmp/outputs/`\\\\n- 2026-05-14 清理后 142G free\\\\n\\\\n## 已删除的 Checkpoint (2026-05-14)\\\\n- plan005_sta_low_s42: D030 决策不重训，用训练曲线数据\\\\n- plan001_s43_r4 和 plan001_s44: 早前清理\\\\n- grpo_exec_only 和 grpo_exec_sta: 早期 100 步实验\\\\n\\\\n## 准备就绪的资产 (2026-05-14)\\\\n\\\\n### Prolog STA\\\\n- 代码+eval 全部就绪，swipl 8.4.2 已验证，PrologSolver proved() 测试通过\\\\n- eval_sta.py 已有完整 Prolog 支持（L11/L33/L87-91/L162-163），不需要补代码\\\\n- plan006 可直接启动（500 步 × 3 seeds, 42/43/44）\\\\n- Director 命令模板：`python3 scripts/train_grpo.py --config configs/grpo_prolog_exec_only.yaml --num_steps 500 --seed {42|43|44} --output_dir ... --warmup_ratio 0.1`\\\\n- K 值等 dry-run 结果定（PR<5% → K=16，否则 K=8）\\\\n\\\\n### 文献\\\\n- literature_map.yaml: 33 篇论文，覆盖 6 个类别\\\\n- literature_goodhart.md: 30+ 篇 Goodhart 专题论文（2026-05-14 Worker 产出）\\\\n\\\\n### Eval 脚本\\\\n- eval_sta.py: FOL + Prolog 双支持，就绪\\\\n- eval_downstream.py: 就绪（需 `--base_model` 参数）\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 9715, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LMWNo8hC9B2rbv7uzY6Tkp","session_id":"89cdeb8e-ead5-4a30-afa3-c65e0c614ccf","uuid":"91d47d6c-9c17-4ef7-9b58-af8e3ce2805e","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:17:26.683Z"}
